ath, I could make a fake npm that creates the runtime bin. Let me think about what's cleaner.\n\nActually, let me look at how the existing tests + the referenced skills suggest doing this. The fake_binary_script_skill says to restore PATH inside the fake script. The pi_runtime_verification_skill section 7 mentions test isolation via HOME temp.\n\nLet me think about the simplest robust approach that tests real behavior:\n\n**Approach A: Fake npm creates real managed runtime structure**\n\nFake `npm` script:\n```sh\n#!/bin/sh\nexport PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin:$PATH\n# args: install --prefix <prefix> --no-fund --no-audit <spec>\n# Create <prefix>/node_modules/.bin/pi\ncase \"$1\" in\n  install)\n    # find --prefix value\n    prefix=\"\"\n    for i in \"$@\"; do\n      case \"$i\" in\n        --prefix) : ;;\n        *) if [ \"$prev\" = \"--prefix\" ]; then prefix=\"$i\"; fi; prev=\"$i\" ;;\n      esac\n    done\n    mkdir -p \"$prefix/node_modules/.bin\"\n    cat > \"$prefix/node_modules/.bin/pi\" <<'EOF'\n#!/bin/sh\nexport PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin:$PATH\ncase \"$1\" in\n  --version) echo \"0.84.1\";;\n  list) echo \"User packages:\"; echo \"  pi-subagents\"; echo \"  pi-web-access\";;\n  install) echo \"installing $2\";;\nesac\nEOF\n    chmod +x \"$prefix/node_modules/.bin/pi\"\n    exit 0\n    ;;\nesac\n```\n\nWait, but this fake npm is what creates the fake pi. But there's a simpler approach: since `installManagedPI` runs `npm install --prefix <prefix> --no-fund --no-audit <spec>`, and the fake npm just needs to exit 0 AND create the RuntimeBin at `<prefix>/node_modules/.bin/pi`.\n\nActually, hmm, this is getting complicated for a Python test. But it's actually the RIGHT test — it tests the real install branch.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety about the fake pi needing to handle `install npm:pi-subagents` and `install npm:pi-web-access`. If the fake pi's `list` always returns \"pi-subagents pi-web-access\", then `ensureNpmExtension` will skip installing them (already registered), so no `install` call happens. But then `verifyExtensions` at the end passes. So actually the fake pi just needs:\n- `--version` → print version\n- `list` → print both extensions\n\nBut wait — after the runtime bin is created by fake npm, the fake pi (at runtime bin) is used for `piCommand` (since `piCommand` prefers `runtime.RuntimeBin()` if exists). So the fake pi at `$prefix/node_modules/.bin/pi` needs to handle `--version`, `list`, and possibly `install`.\n\nActually the flow after install:\n1. `ensurePI()` returns the runtime bin path (newly installed).\n2. `piVersion(piPath)` → `piCommand(\"--version\")` with cmd.Path set to piPath → runs runtime bin `--version` → prints \"0.84.1\".\n3. `bootstrapAgentSettings()` → creates agent dir + settings.json + theme.\n4. `ensureNpmExtension(\"pi-subagents\")` → `piListContains(\"pi-subagents\")` → `piCommand(\"list\")` → runtime bin `list` → prints both → found → skip install.\n5. Same for pi-web-access.\n6. `purgeTokyoNight()` → reads settings.json (fresh, no tokyo) → no-op.\n7. `verifyExtensions()` → `pi list` → both found → empty missing.\n8. `currentTheme()` → \"rick\" (set by bootstrap) → prints theme active.\n9. \"✅ pi environment ready\".\n\nSo the fake pi at runtime bin just needs `--version` and `list` handlers. \n\nThis is doable but a bit involved in Python. Alternatively, I could pre-seed the runtime and put a fake pi at the runtime bin path directly, plus skip the npm install entirely.\n\n**Approach B (simpler): Pre-seed managed runtime**\n\n1. Set HOME to temp dir.\n2. Create `$HOME/.rick/pi/agent/runtime/node_modules/.bin/pi` as a fake pi script that handles `--version` and `list`.\n3. Set PATH to a dir with fake node/npm (but they won't be called since RuntimeBin exists → node check skipped). Actually with RuntimeBin existing, `requireNodeForPiInstall` is skipped, and `ensurePI` returns immediately (FileExists → true), so node/npm/npx are never invoked. So PATH just needs