{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"The exception case already works with the current binary (exit 1, stderr contains \"requires Node.js\"). Wait — the stderr contains \"pi requires Node.js\" not \"requires Node.js\". The test method says: \"stderr 含 `requires Node.js`\". My check should look for \"requires Node.js\" as substring. The actual output is \"pi requires Node.js (>= 22.19.0)...\". So substring \"requires Node.js\" is present. Good.\n\nNote: the current `bin/rick` is an old binary (modified in git). But the test script builds fresh from source.\n\nNow let me verify the normal path works with fake binaries. Let me manually create a fake environment and test. Actually, let me first check whether the normal path with fake binaries works against the CURRENT source (which has init-pi in cmd, not env). Since the task says \"init-pi behavior stays unchanged\", the current source should already produce `✅ pi environment ready` with fake binaries.\n\nLet me set up a quick manual test to confirm my fake binary design works, before writing the Python test.\n\nLet me write a test setup in /tmp and try it.\n\nActually, let me think about the fake npm approach more carefully. The concern: `installManagedPI` runs `npm install --prefix <prefix> --no-fund --no-audit <spec>`. My fake npm needs to:\n1. Parse `--prefix <prefix>`.\n2. Create `<prefix>/node_modules/.bin/pi` (a fake pi script).\n\nBut wait — there might be an issue. `ensurePI` first does `exec.LookPath(\"pi\")` to find global pi version. With PATH pointing to fake dir containing fake pi, it finds fake pi, gets version via `piVersion(p)` = `piCommand(\"--version\")` with cmd.Path = fake pi path. The fake pi on PATH must handle `--version`.\n\nActually wait, let me trace `piVersion`:\n```go\nfunc piVersion(piPath string) string {\n\tcmd := piCommand(\"--version\")\n\tif piPath != \"\" {\n\t\tcmd.Path = piPath\n\t}\n\tout, err := cmd.Output()\n\t...\n}\n```\n`piCommand(\"--version\")` → bin = \"pi\" (RuntimeBin doesn't exist), cmd = exec.Command(\"pi\", \"--version\"), env = AgentEnv. Then cmd.Path = piPath (the fake pi on PATH). So it runs the fake pi `--version`. Good.\n\nSo fake pi on PATH needs `--version` handler (prints version), and also `list`/`install` if called before runtime installed. Actually, before install, `ensureNpmExtension` is called AFTER ensurePI, and by then RuntimeBin exists (created by fake npm). So `piCommand` uses RuntimeBin. So the PATH fake pi only needs `--version` for the version-detection step.\n\nBut to be safe, I'll make the PATH fake pi also handle `list` and `install` (same as runtime fake pi).\n\nActually, simpler: I'll make ONE fake pi script content and use it for both the PATH fake pi and the runtime fake pi (created by fake npm). Both handle `--version`, `list`, `install`.\n\nLet me define the fake pi script (shared):\n```sh\n#!/bin/sh\nexport PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin:$PATH\ncase \"$1\" in\n  --version) echo \"0.84.1\" ;;\n  list) echo \"User packages:\"; echo \"  pi-subagents\"; echo \"  pi-web-access\" ;;\n  install) echo \"installing $2\" ;;\n  *) exit 0 ;;\nesac\nexit 0\n```\n\nThe fake npm script:\n```sh\n#!/bin/sh\nexport PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin:$PATH\nprefix=\"\"\nprev=\"\"\nfor a in \"$@\"; do\n  if [ \"$prev\" = \"--prefix\" ]; then prefix=\"$a\"; fi\n  prev=\"$a\"\ndone\nif [ -z \"$prefix\" ]; then\n  echo \"fake npm: --prefix missing\" >&2\n  exit 1\nfi\nmkdir -p \"$prefix/node_modules/.bin\"\ncat > \"$prefix/node_modules/.bin/pi\" <<'FAKEPI_EOF'\n#!/bin/sh\nexport PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin:$PATH\ncase \"$1\" in\n  --version) echo \"0.84.1\" ;;\n  list) echo \"User packages:\"; echo \"  pi-subagents\"; echo \"  pi-web-access\" ;;\n  install) echo \"installing $2\" ;;\n  *) exit 0 ;;\nesac\nexit 0\nFAKEPI_EOF\nchmod +x \"$prefix/node_modules/.bin/pi\"\nexit 0\n```\n\nfake node / npx: `#!/bin/sh\\nexit 0\\n`.\n\nNow the flow:\n1. HOME = temp, PATH = fake_bin_dir.\n2. `./bin/rick tools init-pi`:\n   - RuntimeBin = $HOME/.rick/pi/agent/runtime/node_modules/.bin/pi → doesn't exist → requireNodeForPiInstall: LookPath(\"node\") → fake node found; LookPath(\"npm\") → fake npm found → nil (pass).\n   - ensurePI: RuntimeBin doesn't exist → LookPath(\"pi\") → fake pi found → piVersion → \"0.84.1\" → print \"installing self-contained pi...\" → installManagedPI(\"0.84.1\") → fake npm creates runtime bin → RuntimeBin exists → return (bin, true).\n   - piVersion(bin) → \"0.84.1\" → print \"✅ rick pi runtime ready: <bin> (v0.84.1) (newly installed)\".\n   - bootstrapAgentSettings → create agent dir, write settings.json (hideThinkingBlock=true, theme=rick), write themes/rick.json, setTheme(\"rick\").\n   - ensureNpmExtension(pi-subagents): piListContains → piCommand(\"list\") → RuntimeBin (exists now) → fake pi list → \"pi-subagents\" found → skip.\n   - ensureNpmExtension(pi-web-access): found → skip.\n   - purgeTokyoNight: settings.json exists, no tokyo → no-op.\n   - verifyExtensions: both found → \"✅ verification: all required extensions registered\".\n   - currentTheme → \"rick\" → \"✅ verification: theme rick active\".\n   - \"✅ pi environment ready\".\n   - exit 0.\n\nThis should work. Let me manually test it now to confirm my design before writing the Python test."}}
