{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Idempotency works: second run has no \"newly installed\". \n\nNow, the current source already implements init-pi behavior fully in cmd. So Test Case 1 (normal path) and Test Case 2 idempotency (part A) and Test Case 3 (exception, node/npm) would all PASS with current code. Only the `go test ./internal/env/...` part fails (env package doesn't exist).\n\nSo my Python test script will currently produce:\n- Test 1 normal path: PASS (no error added)\n- Test 2 idempotency: PASS (no \"newly installed\", exit 0)\n- Test 2 env check `go test ./internal/env/... -run TestIsPIReady`: FAIL (package doesn't exist / test not found)\n- Test 3 exception node/npm: PASS\n- Test 3 `CheckEcosystemExtensions()` missing: this is a Go function — but it's not accessible from Python directly. It's verified via `go test ./internal/env/...` (a Go test). Since env doesn't exist, this also fails (covered by the same go test failure).\n\nSo overall: pass=false, with errors about internal/env not existing / TestIsPIReady not found.\n\nThis is the correct RED phase: the test fails because `internal/env` (the thing task3 must create) doesn't exist yet.\n\nNow, importantly: my test script should be designed so that AFTER task3 is implemented, it passes. The key question: how does my Python test verify the \"four check functions\" and \"CheckEcosystemExtensions returns non-empty when extension missing\"?\n\nThe test method says these are verified via `go test ./internal/env/... -run TestIsPIReady -v` and the Go tests that task3 will write. So my Python test runs `go test ./internal/env/...` and checks it passes.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety. The test method for Test Case 2 says the go test should verify `IsPIReady()` returns ok=true with all four check functions ready. And Test Case 3 says \"另测 `CheckEcosystemExtensions()` 在某扩展缺失时返回非空切片\". These are Go-level tests that task3's implementer writes as part of the env package (`TestIsPIReady` and presumably a test for `CheckEcosystemExtensions`).\n\nSo my Python test should:\n1. Run `go test ./internal/env/... -run TestIsPIReady -v` → expect pass (exit 0, \"ok\" / \"PASS\").\n2. For the \"CheckEcosystemExtensions returns non-empty when missing\" — is there a specific Go test name? The test method doesn't name a specific test. It says \"另测 CheckEcosystemExtensions() 在某扩展缺失时返回非空切片\". \n\nHmm. The Python test can't call the Go function directly. Options:\n(a) Run the full `go test ./internal/env/...` (all tests) and expect pass — this would include whatever test the implementer writes for CheckEcosystemExtensions.\n(b) Run a specific test name if known.\n\nThe test method for Test Case 2 explicitly names `-run TestIsPIReady`. For Test Case 3's \"CheckEcosystemExtensions returns non-empty\", it doesn't name a specific Go test. So I should run the full `go test ./internal/env/...` package test suite and check it passes, which covers both TestIsPIReady and any CheckEcosystemExtensions tests the implementer writes.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The test method describes 3 test cases, each with 操作 and 预期. The Python script needs to cover:\n1. Normal path: build + run init-pi, check exit 0 + \"✅ pi environment ready\".\n2. Boundary: run init-pi again (idempotent, no \"newly installed\") + `go test ./internal/env/... -run TestIsPIReady -v` passes.\n3. Exception: run init-pi with empty PATH → stderr \"requires Node.js\", exit 1, no panic. Plus CheckEcosystemExtensions returns non-empty when extension missing (via go test).\n\nFor #3's \"CheckEcosystemExtensions returns non-empty\", I'll run the full env package test suite `go test ./internal/env/...` and expect it to pass (which means the implementer's test for CheckEcosystemExtensions passes). But that's a bit indirect. \n\nActually, a cleaner interpretation: The Python test script's job is to verify the OUTCOME (the test method's 预期). The Go-level functions (IsPIReady, CheckEcosystemExtensions) are tested by Go tests that task3 writes. The Python test runs `go test ./internal/env/...` and checks it passes. That's the standard pattern here — looking at how existing tests in this repo work, and the task4 testgen prompt (similar).\n\nLet me check the task4 testgen prompt to see the pattern used for a similar task (runtime migration)."}}
