ion that checks `runtime.RuntimeBin()` on the real ~/.rick/pi), it might PASS or FAIL depending on the real machine state (real ~/.rick/pi/agent exists on this machine with runtime installed). That would make the test machine-dependent. But that's the implementer's bug, and 关键结果 #5 requires isolated HOME testing. My job is to write the test script; the implementer must make `go test` green in isolation.\n\nTo be robust and avoid machine-dependence in MY test, I should run `go test ./internal/env/...` with an isolated HOME (temp dir) so it doesn't accidentally hit the real ~/.rick/pi. But wait — if I isolate HOME, then a naive non-isolating Go test would check `$HOME/.rick/pi/agent/runtime` in the temp dir → not installed → IsPIReady false → test fails. But that's actually CORRECT behavior for a test that should set up its own fake env. Hmm, but if the implementer writes TestIsPIReady to set up its own fake env (t.Setenv), then my passing an isolated HOME is harmless (their t.Setenv overrides).\n\nActually, the cleanest: run `go test ./internal/env/...` with `env` = a copy of os.environ but with HOME pointing to a fresh temp dir. This ensures:\n1. If the Go tests properly isolate → unaffected (they override HOME).\n2. If a Go test forgets to isolate → it checks a temp HOME → fails deterministically (not machine-dependent) → catches the bug.\n\nWait, but that could cause a false failure if the implementer intends `TestIsPIReady` to run against the ACTUAL deployed fake env (from the Python script). But Go tests can't easily access the Python script's fake env (they'd need it passed). The standard Go testing pattern in this repo is self-isolation. So running with isolated HOME is the right call.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider. Let me look at how the existing tests do it. `tools_init_pi_test.go` uses `t.Setenv(\"HOME\", t.TempDir())` and `writeFakePi`. So the Go tests are fully self-isolating. When task3 migrates these tests to env, they'll keep the same pattern. So