n Linux, `$HOME/.cache/go-build`. If I change HOME, the go build cache is recreated (slower first run), but it still works. Actually, `go test` after `go build` (which used default HOME) would use a DIFFERENT cache if HOME differs. This could slow things but not break.\n\nWait, actually there's a real concern: if I set `HOME` to a temp dir for `go test`, Go will use `$TEMP/go-build` or `$HOME/.cache/go-build`? Let me recall: Go determines cache dir via `os.UserCacheDir()` which on Linux uses `$XDG_CACHE_HOME` or `$HOME/.cache`. So changing HOME changes the cache dir. First `go test` with new HOME → recompiles everything (slow). But it still works. To avoid this slowdown AND avoid affecting correctness, I could NOT change HOME for go test, and instead rely on the Go tests' own `t.Setenv(\"HOME\", ...)`.\n\nHmm, but then if a Go test forgets to isolate, it hits real ~/.rick/pi. But that's the implementer's responsibility and 关键结果 #5 requires green tests with isolation.\n\nTrade-off:\n- Set HOME=temp for go test: safer isolation, but slower (cache miss) and might cause unrelated issues (e.g., go needing HOME for something).\n- Don't set HOME: faster, but if tests don't self-isolate, machine-dependent.\n\nActually, looking at task4.py: it runs `go test` with DEFAULT env (no env override for go test). And task4's tests self-isolate via t.Setenv. So the established pattern is: run go test with default env, trust Go tests to isolate.\n\nI'll follow task4.py: run go test with default env (no HOME override). This is consistent and faster. The Go tests (migrated from tools_init_pi_test.go) all use t.Setenv(\"HOME\", t.TempDir()), so they'll self-isolate.\n\nWait, but there's a subtle risk: `go test ./internal/env/...` — if the env package has an integration test that does NOT isolate and tries to hit real pi... but again, implementer's responsibility, and the existing test suite already passes in this repo's CI.\n\nDecision: run `go test` with default env (no override), matching task4.py. Simpler and consistent.\n\nActually, hmm, wait. Let me reconsider once more. There's a real risk: after task3, the env package tests might include `TestIsPIReady` which, to test \"ok=true\", sets up a fake env. If the implementer writes it correctly (like existing tests), it self-isolates. Fine.\n\nBut what if `TestIsPIReady` is written to check the REAL deployed state (expecting the CLI test to have deployed things)? That would be a bad test (machine-dependent), and would fail in CI. The 关键结果 #5 says \"隔离 HOME 下 rick tools init-pi 全 ✅\" and \"go test ./internal/env/... ./internal/cmd/... 全绿\" as SEPARATE requirements, implying go test is self-contained. So self-isolation is expected.\n\nFinal decision: default env for go test.\n\nNow let me also reconsider the go test timeout. `go test ./internal/env/...` after task3 might run fake-binary tests (fast, <1s each) + possibly some integration. 120s is plenty. I'll use `-timeout 120s` and subprocess timeout 180s.\n\nNow, about the build: I'll build once and reuse for all CLI tests. Good.\n\nLet me also think: should the build failure short-circuit? Yes — if build fails, the CLI tests can't run meaningfully (no binary). I'll short-circuit on build failure (return early), because subsequent CLI tests would all fail with \"binary not found\" noise. But go test / structural checks could still run. Actually, if build fails, the env package likely doesn't compile either (go test would also fail). To keep it simple and informative, on build failure I'll still run the go test + structural checks? Hmm. task4.py collects build failure as an error but CONTINUES (doesn't short-circuit) — it appends error and moves on. For task3, if build fails, running init-pi would fail (binary missing). I'll guard the CLI tests: only run them if build succeeded (rick_bin exists). This avoids cascading noise.\n\nLet me structure:\n```python\nbuild_ok = (rc == 0) and os.path.isfile(rick_bin)\nif rc != 0: errors.append('build failed...