have identical content. I'll define FAKE_PI as one constant and use it in write_fake_bin for the PATH 'pi', and embed it in FAKE_NPM's heredoc.\n\nWait — but the FAKE_NPM heredoc content must match FAKE_PI exactly. To avoid duplication drift, I could generate FAKE_NPM by embedding FAKE_PI. Let me do that programmatically:\n\n```python\nFAKE_PI = \"\"\"#!/bin/sh\nexport PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin:$PATH\ncase \"$1\" in\n  --version) echo \"0.84.1\" ;;\n  list) echo \"User packages:\"; echo \"  pi-subagents\"; echo \"  pi-web-access\" ;;\n  install) echo \"installing $2\" ;;\n  *) exit 0 ;;\nesac\nexit 0\n\"\"\"\n\nFAKE_NPM = \"\"\"#!/bin/sh\nexport PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin:$PATH\nprefix=\"\"\nprev=\"\"\nfor a in \"$@\"; do\n  if [ \"$prev\" = \"--prefix\" ]; then prefix=\"$a\"; fi\n  prev=\"$a\"\ndone\nif [ -z \"$prefix\" ]; then\n  echo \"fake npm: --prefix missing\" >&2\n  exit 1\nfi\nmkdir -p \"$prefix/node_modules/.bin\"\ncat > \"$prefix/node_modules/.bin/pi\" <<'FAKEPI_EOF'\n\"\"\" + FAKE_PI + \"\"\"\nFAKEPI_EOF\nchmod +x \"$prefix/node_modules/.bin/pi\"\nexit 0\n\"\"\"\n```\n\nThis is clean. The FAKE_PI content is embedded verbatim inside the heredoc.\n\nNow, `write_fake_bin`:\n```python\ndef write_fake_bin(dirpath, name, content):\n    path = os.path.join(dirpath, name)\n    with open(path, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        f.write(content)\n    os.chmod(path, 0o755)\n```\n\nFor node/npx: content = '#!/bin/sh\\nexit 0\\n'.\n\nNow let me also double check: the fake npm's `--prefix` parsing. `installManagedPI` calls:\n`exec.Command(\"npm\", \"install\", \"--prefix\", prefix, \"--no-fund\", \"--no-audit\", spec)`\n\nSo argv = [\"npm\", \"install\", \"--prefix\", \"/tmp/.../runtime\", \"--no-fund\", \"--no-audit\", \"spec\"]. My loop:\n- a=\"install\", prev=\"\" → no match\n- a=\"--prefix\", prev=\"install\" → no match (prev != \"--prefix\"), then prev=\"--prefix\"\n- a=prefix, prev=\"--prefix\" → prefix=a ✓, prev=prefix\n- a=\"--no-fund\", pr