pi on PATH AND set HOME to temp dir, then `RuntimeBin()` = `$HOME/.rick/pi/agent/runtime/node_modules/.bin/pi` which doesn't exist → `piCommand` falls back to \"pi\" on PATH (fake pi). Good.\n\nBut `requireNodeForPiInstall` checks `exec.LookPath(\"node\")` and `exec.LookPath(\"npm\")` — these only run if `RuntimeBin()` does NOT exist. So I need fake node + npm on PATH too (or pre-seed runtime). So PATH should contain fake node, npm, npx, and pi.\n\nActually wait, let me re-read: the node check runs `if !runtime.FileExists(runtime.RuntimeBin())`. Since HOME is temp, RuntimeBin doesn't exist → check runs → needs node+npm on PATH. So provide fake node, npm, npx.\n\nThen `ensurePI()`: RuntimeBin doesn't exist → looks for \"pi\" on PATH (fake) → `piVersion(p)` = \"0.84.1\" → installManagedPI(\"0.84.1\") runs `npm install --prefix ... @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent@0.84.1`. The fake npm must succeed and actually create the RuntimeBin at `$prefix/node_modules/.bin/pi`!\n\nHmm, this is getting complex. The fake npm needs to create the managed runtime structure so that `ensurePI` finds the binary after install. That's the \"real behavior\" the test should exercise.\n\nAlternatively, use option (b): pre-seed the managed runtime so node check is skipped. This is simpler and still tests the real branches (bootstrap settings, ensure extensions, purge, verify). The task method explicitly allows: \"或先预置 managed runtime 使 node 检查跳过\" (or pre-seed managed runtime to skip node check).\n\nBut actually, for the test to be robust and test the REAL install path, I could make a fake npm that creates the runtime bin. Let me think about what's cleaner.\n\nActually, let me look at how the existing tests + the referenced skills suggest doing this. The fake_binary_script_skill says to restore PATH inside the fake script. The pi_runtime_verification_skill section 7 mentions test isolation via HOME temp.\n\nLet me think about the simplest robust approach that tests real behavior:\n\n**Approach 