o test (TestIsPIReady) itself will be written by task3 implementer. My Python test just needs to run it and check it passes. The Go test should set up its own env (it's a unit test in the env package). But the `CheckPIInstalled`/`CheckEcosystemExtensions`/`CheckRickAgents`/`CheckRickHooks` functions depend on the env state. For `TestIsPIReady` to return ok=true, it needs the env to be \"ready\" (pi installed, extensions present, rick agents/hooks deployed).\n\nHmm, this is where it gets tricky. `IsPIReady()` checks:\n- CheckPIInstalled: pi binary exists (runtime bin or PATH pi)\n- CheckEcosystemExtensions: pi list contains extensions\n- CheckRickAgents: rick agents deployed to agent dir\n- CheckRickHooks: rick hooks deployed\n\nFor these to be ready in the Go test, the Go test needs to set up a fake env. This is the implementer's job. The Python test just runs `go test ./internal/env/... -run TestIsPIReady -v` and checks exit 0.\n\nBut should the Python test set HOME/PATH env when running the go test? If it does, and the go test relies on `t.Setenv` internally (which it should for isolation), then it's fine. If the go test expects the OUTER env to be the pre-seeded one... no, that's bad design. The go test should be self-contained.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The test method says \"前置条件 = init-pi 已成功一次\". This might imply the go test runs against the same isolated HOME where init-pi was run. But that couples the go test to the Python test's env. Better: the go test sets up its own fake env internally (like existing tests do with `t.Setenv`). So the Python test just runs `go test` and checks pass.\n\nI'll run `go test ./internal/env/... -run TestIsPIReady -v` with a clean isolated HOME (so it doesn't accidentally hit real ~/.rick/pi — actually go test with `t.Setenv` handles this; but if the go test is NOT yet written, the command fails at \"no test files\" or \"build failed\").\n\nLet me now actually check what `go test ./internal/env/...` outputs right now (env